---
import RelatedGuides from '../components/RelatedGuides.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const salaryRows = [
  { salary: '$60,000', under50: '42.5%', age50: '56.7%', age60to63: '62.1%' },
  { salary: '$80,000', under50: '31.9%', age50: '42.5%', age60to63: '46.6%' },
  { salary: '$100,000', under50: '25.5%', age50: '34.0%', age60to63: '37.3%' },
  { salary: '$125,000', under50: '20.4%', age50: '27.2%', age60to63: '29.8%' },
  { salary: '$150,000', under50: '17.0%', age50: '22.7%', age60to63: '24.8%' },
  { salary: '$200,000', under50: '12.8%', age50: '17.0%', age60to63: '18.6%' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Much 401(k) Per Paycheck to Max Out in 2027"
  description="Exact per-paycheck 401(k) dollar amounts and salary percentages to max out $25,500, $34,000, or $37,250 in 2027."
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a> / <span>How Much Per Paycheck to Max Out in 2027</span>
  </nav>

  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How Much 401(k) Per Paycheck to Max Out in 2027</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> For a standard 26-paycheck biweekly schedule, maxing out the $25,500 limit takes <strong>$980.76</strong> per paycheck for 25 paychecks and <strong>$981.00</strong> on the final paycheck (or $1,307.69 for age 50+, and $1,432.69 for ages 60–63).
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Customize for Your Remaining Paychecks →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>What Percentage of Salary Maxes Out 2027 Limits?</h2>
    <p>
      Many payroll portals only let you enter a whole-number percentage rather than a dollar amount. Here is the approximate percentage of annual gross pay needed to reach the 2027 limits:
    </p>
    <div tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Salary Percentage to Max Out 2027 401(k)" class="table-wrapper" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Annual Gross Salary</th>
            <th>Under 50 ($25,500)</th>
            <th>50–59 &amp; 64+ ($34,000)</th>
            <th>Ages 60–63 ($37,250)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {salaryRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.salary}</th>
              <td data-label="Under 50 ($25,500)">{row.under50}</td>
              <td data-label="50–59 & 64+ ($34,000)">{row.age50}</td>
              <td data-label="Ages 60–63 ($37,250)">{row.age60to63}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="hint">
      Note: If your payroll system only accepts whole percentages, rounding up by 1% can cause you to hit the cap before December—check <a href="/401k-match-true-up/">whether your plan has an employer match true-up</a> before front-loading.
    </p>

    <h2>Why We Round Down to the Cent (Never $980.77 × 26)</h2>
    <p>
      Dividing $25,500 by 26 gives $980.76923…. Rounding up to $980.77 × 26 equals $25,500.02—two cents over the statutory limit. Rounding the regular paycheck down to $980.76 and adjusting the final paycheck to $981.00 hits $25,500.00 to the penny. See also <a href="/27-paychecks-401k-2027/">how 27-paycheck years work in 2027</a>.
    </p>
  </article>

  <RelatedGuides excludeHref="/how-much-401k-per-paycheck-to-max-out-2027/" />
</BaseLayout>
